# Physics Core Sequence
#
# The undergraduate physics major as a prerequisite graph, from mechanics and waves through quantum and modern physics, with the advanced-lab battery where students measure constants themselves.

#>physics_core
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!physics
#:form #!curriculum
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Physics Core Sequence"
 ~#claim: "An undergraduate physics major climbs mechanics, electromagnetism, quantum mechanics, and thermal physics, and in the advanced lab measures h, e, and the muon lifetime with its own hands."
 ~#proof: "Phys21 for the program shape; Taylor, Griffiths, and Schroeder for the core; Melissinos and Napolitano for the lab battery; Noether 1918 for the symmetry theorem."
 ~#door: "Time a pendulum first, then pick any lab rung and follow its needs back to the core."
 ~#audience: "prospective and current physics majors, and anyone mapping what a physics degree contains"
 continuity: "Physics Core Sequence | with its own hands"
 cold_open: "A physics degree climbs from falling apples to quantum spin, and the last labs measure nature's constants with its own hands."
}

